WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.592 --> 00:00:03.168
Designing a hairpin winding.

2
00:00:03.168 --> 00:00:06.116
Lecture two of the Manufacturing module.

3
00:00:07.224 --> 00:00:09.131
Lecture one showed why hairpins win.

4
00:00:09.131 --> 00:00:12.048
But a hairpin stator is not wound;

5
00:00:12.048 --> 00:00:14.520
it is assembled from rigid pins.

6
00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:19.588
So the winding is designed pin by pin, before any copper is cut.

7
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:27.540
We use our own Hairpin Motor Designer, on the 24-slot, 4-pole core from lecture

8
00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:28.340
one.

9
00:00:27.895 --> 00:00:32.548
By the end you will read a winding diagram:

10
00:00:32.548 --> 00:00:36.572
layers, pitch, special pins, welds, jumpers and parallel paths.

11
00:00:37.538 --> 00:00:38.867
Chapter one.

12
00:00:38.867 --> 00:00:40.014
Pins and layers.

13
00:00:41.598 --> 00:00:44.045
A hairpin is one bar bent into a U.

14
00:00:44.045 --> 00:00:46.129
The bend is the crown.

15
00:00:46.129 --> 00:00:51.275
The two legs pass through two slots and come out at the far end,

16
00:00:51.275 --> 00:00:52.906
where they are twisted and welded.

17
00:00:53.854 --> 00:00:56.538
Each slot has an even number of bar positions:

18
00:00:56.538 --> 00:00:57.506
the layers.

19
00:00:57.506 --> 00:01:02.562
We number them from the bore, layer one nearest the rotor.

20
00:01:02.562 --> 00:01:03.754
Our slot has four.

21
00:01:04.990 --> 00:01:07.197
A pin always joins two neighbouring layers:

22
00:01:07.197 --> 00:01:10.680
one and two, or three and four.

23
00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:13.034
So four layers make two layer pairs.

24
00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:15.138
Now count.

25
00:01:15.138 --> 00:01:18.758
Twenty-four slots times four layers is ninety-six bars.

26
00:01:18.758 --> 00:01:22.858
Each pin has two legs, so forty-eight hairpins.

27
00:01:23.582 --> 00:01:25.607
Each pin is one turn.

28
00:01:25.607 --> 00:01:31.081
So the turns per phase are the layers, times the pole pairs p, times

29
00:01:31.081 --> 00:01:35.837
q, the slots per pole per phase, over the parallel paths a.

30
00:01:35.837 --> 00:01:36.637
Here:

31
00:01:36.552 --> 00:01:39.923
four times two times two, over one.

32
00:01:39.923 --> 00:01:40.723
Sixteen.

33
00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:43.452
More layers, more turns:

34
00:01:43.452 --> 00:01:46.822
six layers give 24, eight give 32.

35
00:01:46.822 --> 00:01:54.305
But the bars get thinner, the fill falls from 0.72 to 0.64, and the

36
00:01:54.305 --> 00:02:00.533
crown grows from 51.5 to 63.4 millimetres, because more legs must twist past each

37
00:02:00.533 --> 00:02:01.333
other.

38
00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:03.479
The welds follow:

39
00:02:03.479 --> 00:02:06.410
42 with four layers, 90 with eight.

40
00:02:11.457 --> 00:02:12.257
Chapter two.

41
00:02:12.145 --> 00:02:13.645
Pitch and twist.

42
00:02:14.973 --> 00:02:17.760
The pole pitch is the slots per pole:

43
00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:19.305
24 over 4 is 6.

44
00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:24.595
For the voltages in a pin's two legs to add, the legs must sit

45
00:02:24.595 --> 00:02:26.684
about one pole pitch apart.

46
00:02:26.684 --> 00:02:29.257
So our pins span 6 slots at the crown.

47
00:02:30.301 --> 00:02:33.904
At the weld end each leg is bent sideways three slots:

48
00:02:33.904 --> 00:02:36.936
odd layers one way, even layers the other.

49
00:02:36.936 --> 00:02:42.473
Two legs that started six slots apart meet in the middle, ready to weld.

50
00:02:43.261 --> 00:02:47.303
So each pin and its weld carry the path twelve slots on:

51
00:02:47.303 --> 00:02:48.103
two poles.

52
00:02:47.852 --> 00:02:51.851
Two pins take it once round the stator.

53
00:02:51.851 --> 00:02:53.225
This is a wave winding.

54
00:02:53.885 --> 00:02:57.603
You can shorten the crown pitch to five slots.

55
00:02:57.603 --> 00:03:02.893
The weld side must then span seven to keep the twelve.

56
00:03:02.893 --> 00:03:08.383
On our core the crown drops from 51.5 to 46.4 millimetres, and the weld

57
00:03:08.383 --> 00:03:10.889
end grows from 77.1 to 87.4.

58
00:03:11.773 --> 00:03:13.582
Why do it?

59
00:03:13.582 --> 00:03:19.455
The winding factor is the share of the pin voltages that adds up.

60
00:03:19.455 --> 00:03:26.178
Short pitch costs little at the fundamental, 0.966 to 0.933, but cuts the fifth

61
00:03:26.178 --> 00:03:29.485
and seventh harmonics from 0.259 to 0.067.

62
00:03:29.485 --> 00:03:35.337
An early hairpin patent recommends about five sixths of a pole pitch.

63
00:03:36.125 --> 00:03:37.243
The price:

64
00:03:37.243 --> 00:03:40.046
some slots now hold two phases.

65
00:03:40.046 --> 00:03:45.746
In our model the worst voltage between neighbouring bars rises from 0.88 to 1.58

66
00:03:45.746 --> 00:03:47.354
times the phase peak.

67
00:03:47.354 --> 00:03:50.125
The insulation has to hold that;

68
00:03:50.125 --> 00:03:52.233
lecture five tests it.

69
00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:54.758
Chapter three.

70
00:03:54.758 --> 00:03:55.650
Special pins.

71
00:03:57.106 --> 00:04:01.819
With q equal to two, each phase owns two neighbouring slots under every pole.

72
00:04:01.819 --> 00:04:07.192
But a wave of twelve-slot steps goes once round and lands back in its

73
00:04:07.192 --> 00:04:08.541
own slot.

74
00:04:08.541 --> 00:04:09.790
The neighbour stays empty.

75
00:04:10.482 --> 00:04:13.642
So some pins are made one slot longer or shorter.

76
00:04:13.642 --> 00:04:18.800
In our design a seven-slot pin and a five-slot pin sit nested in one

77
00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:19.600
belt.

78
00:04:19.095 --> 00:04:24.734
The path steps one slot over, and the next lap fills the neighbouring slot.

79
00:04:25.778 --> 00:04:27.890
Patents and papers use the same trick:

80
00:04:27.890 --> 00:04:32.222
short and long-pitch pins, or a shorter connection at the weld side.

81
00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:36.155
Three more special parts close the path.

82
00:04:36.155 --> 00:04:40.664
Switched welds join layer two to layer three, moving the path to the next

83
00:04:40.664 --> 00:04:42.011
layer pair.

84
00:04:42.011 --> 00:04:44.899
One jumper per phase turns it back.

85
00:04:44.899 --> 00:04:48.702
And six leads, U1 to W2, go to the terminal box.

86
00:04:49.554 --> 00:04:53.521
Each special part is another shape, and another tool.

87
00:04:53.521 --> 00:04:58.297
Our stator needs six pin shapes, three jumpers and six leads;

88
00:04:58.297 --> 00:05:02.658
one published 48 and 54-slot design used seven to ten pin shapes.

89
00:05:02.658 --> 00:05:08.030
Jumpers also add material and a step, so designers keep them few.

90
00:05:13.164 --> 00:05:14.538
Chapter four.

91
00:05:14.538 --> 00:05:15.704
Following one phase.

92
00:05:17.192 --> 00:05:19.724
Open the trace for phase U.

93
00:05:19.724 --> 00:05:24.396
The current enters at lead U1, into slot one, layer four, at the bottom

94
00:05:24.396 --> 00:05:25.268
of the slot.

95
00:05:26.248 --> 00:05:30.431
It waves round in layers four and three, two laps:

96
00:05:30.431 --> 00:05:35.417
slots 1, 19, 13 and 8, then 2, 20, 14 and 7.

97
00:05:35.417 --> 00:05:38.612
The special pins make the one-slot step.

98
00:05:39.816 --> 00:05:44.096
Then a switched weld takes it down to layers two and one, where it

99
00:05:44.096 --> 00:05:45.428
makes the same two laps.

100
00:05:45.992 --> 00:05:50.589
In layer one it meets the jumper, which turns it round.

101
00:05:50.589 --> 00:05:55.482
It runs back through layers one and two, then three and four, and leaves

102
00:05:55.482 --> 00:05:57.417
at lead U2.

103
00:05:57.417 --> 00:05:58.925
Thirty-two bars in series:

104
00:05:58.925 --> 00:05:59.725
sixteen turns.

105
00:06:00.424 --> 00:06:02.789
The Designer checks every phase:

106
00:06:02.789 --> 00:06:09.282
each bar used once, the current direction right in every slot, three equal voltages

107
00:06:09.282 --> 00:06:10.420
120 degrees apart.

108
00:06:11.496 --> 00:06:16.175
For half the voltage and twice the current, use two parallel paths.

109
00:06:16.175 --> 00:06:20.828
Leave out the jumper, and the forward and return halves become two branches of

110
00:06:20.828 --> 00:06:21.748
eight turns each.

111
00:06:22.664 --> 00:06:24.208
Parallel paths must be twins:

112
00:06:24.208 --> 00:06:28.766
each one in every layer and every slot of its belt.

113
00:06:28.766 --> 00:06:31.874
Otherwise their voltages differ, and current circulates between them.

114
00:06:31.874 --> 00:06:35.389
One study swapped two bars in a slot:

115
00:06:35.389 --> 00:06:40.052
at one kilohertz, the loss in one of them rose 88 percent.

116
00:06:41.228 --> 00:06:42.525
Chapter five.

117
00:06:42.525 --> 00:06:44.120
Changing slots and poles.

118
00:06:45.768 --> 00:06:48.905
Now double the slots on the same core:

119
00:06:48.905 --> 00:06:52.597
48 instead of 24, still four poles and four layers.

120
00:06:52.597 --> 00:06:55.796
The teeth get thinner, the slots narrower.

121
00:06:56.456 --> 00:06:58.184
Pins and welds double:

122
00:06:58.184 --> 00:06:59.721
96 pins, 90 welds.

123
00:06:59.721 --> 00:07:02.707
So do the turns, to 32.

124
00:07:02.707 --> 00:07:06.356
To keep our sixteen, use two parallel paths.

125
00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:12.668
The bars shrink from 5.7 to 2.2 millimetres wide, but the liner and clearances

126
00:07:12.668 --> 00:07:14.780
across the slot stay 0.9 millimetres.

127
00:07:14.780 --> 00:07:19.748
In the wide slot that is 14 percent of the width;

128
00:07:19.748 --> 00:07:22.540
in the narrow one, 29.

129
00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:27.904
The fill falls from 0.72 to 0.59, and at the same phase current the

130
00:07:27.904 --> 00:07:30.196
DC copper loss rises 39 percent.

131
00:07:30.984 --> 00:07:32.782
The crown hardly moves:

132
00:07:32.782 --> 00:07:34.208
51.5 millimetres, then 50.7.

133
00:07:34.208 --> 00:07:40.343
A pin still spans one pole pitch, the same arc on this bore.

134
00:07:40.343 --> 00:07:42.749
Change the poles instead:

135
00:07:42.749 --> 00:07:48.436
at eight poles the pole pitch halves, and the crown drops to 33.2.

136
00:07:49.192 --> 00:07:53.264
Eight poles have a price you met in lecture one.

137
00:07:53.264 --> 00:07:55.836
At the same speed the frequency doubles:

138
00:07:55.836 --> 00:08:00.724
at 12,000 rpm, four poles run at 400 hertz, eight poles at 800.

139
00:08:01.769 --> 00:08:07.487
We ran FEMM, the finite-element program from lecture one, on one 48-slot slot.

140
00:08:07.487 --> 00:08:14.267
The narrow bars fill less of the slot width, so the current crowds less:

141
00:08:14.267 --> 00:08:20.663
the loss ratio K, AC over DC loss, is 2.37 at one kilohertz, against

142
00:08:20.663 --> 00:08:22.485
3.45 with 24 slots.

143
00:08:23.177 --> 00:08:24.506
And heat.

144
00:08:24.506 --> 00:08:30.258
At the same phase current the 48-slot hot spot rises 10.1 kelvin, against 10.5

145
00:08:30.258 --> 00:08:31.571
with 24 slots.

146
00:08:31.571 --> 00:08:35.765
Less copper, but a shorter path to the tooth.

147
00:08:36.681 --> 00:08:37.481
One surprise.

148
00:08:37.289 --> 00:08:41.874
Put round wire in the same narrow slot, packed like our kit:

149
00:08:41.874 --> 00:08:47.311
the fill is 0.39, and per watt its hot spot is within two percent

150
00:08:47.311 --> 00:08:48.755
of the bars.

151
00:08:48.755 --> 00:08:51.852
The heat path is short either way.

152
00:08:51.852 --> 00:08:53.372
Hairpins keep their copper advantage;

153
00:08:53.372 --> 00:08:56.053
most of the heat advantage is gone.

154
00:08:57.545 --> 00:09:02.990
EV motors often use 48 slots and 8 poles, which one design guide calls

155
00:09:02.990 --> 00:09:04.066
the traditional choice.

156
00:09:04.066 --> 00:09:11.151
Their bore is set by the torque they need, which grows with the bore

157
00:09:11.151 --> 00:09:12.765
squared times the length.

158
00:09:12.765 --> 00:09:16.737
On a bore that size the slots stay wide:

159
00:09:16.737 --> 00:09:22.650
our EV-size version, 134 millimetres, has 4.5 millimetre slots, a fill of 0.71 and

160
00:09:22.650 --> 00:09:24.085
a 37.6 millimetre crown.

161
00:09:29.264 --> 00:09:30.149
Summary.

162
00:09:30.149 --> 00:09:31.580
Four points.

163
00:09:32.780 --> 00:09:33.580
One.

164
00:09:33.117 --> 00:09:34.936
Layers set the turns.

165
00:09:34.936 --> 00:09:39.198
Our 24 slots and four layers make 48 pins and 16 turns;

166
00:09:39.198 --> 00:09:43.128
more layers mean thinner bars and a taller crown.

167
00:09:43.852 --> 00:09:44.695
Two.

168
00:09:44.695 --> 00:09:46.869
The crown spans about one pole pitch.

169
00:09:46.869 --> 00:09:51.832
Shortening it trims the harmonics and the crown, and lengthens the weld end.

170
00:09:52.428 --> 00:09:53.228
Three.

171
00:09:53.202 --> 00:09:58.951
Special pins, switched welds, jumpers and leads close the path, and every one is

172
00:09:58.951 --> 00:10:00.888
another shape and another tool.

173
00:10:01.548 --> 00:10:02.348
Four.

174
00:10:02.330 --> 00:10:03.587
Slots and poles trade.

175
00:10:03.587 --> 00:10:04.683
More slots:

176
00:10:04.683 --> 00:10:06.992
more pins and welds, less fill.

177
00:10:06.992 --> 00:10:08.121
More poles:

178
00:10:08.121 --> 00:10:10.744
a lower crown and a higher frequency.

179
00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:14.225
That is the end of lecture two.

180
00:10:14.225 --> 00:10:15.460
Next, lecture three:

181
00:10:15.460 --> 00:10:18.900
from wire to pins, how flat wire becomes a formed hairpin.

182
00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:22.776
Until then, trace phase U yourself in the free Viewer.

183
00:10:23.436 --> 00:10:28.116
Motoreka is a product of UMPSA, developed at EMDLab.

184
00:10:28.116 --> 00:10:30.072
motoreka.my.

